Problem 2 is merge intervals. Sort by start, then sweep: if the next start is at or below the current end, extend the end to the max of the two, otherwise push the current interval and start a new one.

```python
def merge_intervals(intervals):
    if not intervals:
        return []
    intervals.sort(key=lambda pair: pair[0])
    merged = [list(intervals[0])]
    for start, end in intervals[1:]:
        if start <= merged[-1][1]:
            if end > merged[-1][1]:
                merged[-1][1] = end
        else:
            merged.append([start, end])
    return merged
```

Sorting dominates, so it's **O(n log n)** time and O(n) space for the output. Touching intervals merge because the check is `start <= current_end`, not `<`.

[[GIST]] sort by start, sweep and merge

Got it, ties break by first appearance in the input, not alphabetically. So I'll count with a map, then keep a min-heap of size k ordered by count and, on a tie, by the earliest index that word first appeared. That gives O(n log k) time and O(k) extra space.

```javascript
function topKWords(words, k) {
  const count = new Map();
  const firstSeen = new Map();
  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    const w = words[i];
    count.set(w, (count.get(w) || 0) + 1);
    if (!firstSeen.has(w)) firstSeen.set(w, i);
  }

  // "Worse" = lower count, or equal count and later first appearance.
  const worse = (a, b) =>
    count.get(a) !== count.get(b)
      ? count.get(a) < count.get(b)
      : firstSeen.get(a) > firstSeen.get(b);

  const heap = [];
  const swap = (i, j) => { [heap[i], heap[j]] = [heap[j], heap[i]]; };
  const siftUp = (i) => {
    while (i > 0) {
      const p = (i - 1) >> 1;
      if (!worse(heap[i], heap[p])) break;
      swap(i, p);
      i = p;
    }
  };
  const siftDown = (i) => {
    for (;;) {
      let m = i;
      const l = 2 * i + 1, r = 2 * i + 2;
      if (l < heap.length && worse(heap[l], heap[m])) m = l;
      if (r < heap.length && worse(heap[r], heap[m])) m = r;
      if (m === i) break;
      swap(i, m);
      i = m;
    }
  };

  for (const w of count.keys()) {
    heap.push(w);
    siftUp(heap.length - 1);
    if (heap.length > k) {
      heap[0] = heap.pop();
      siftDown(0);
    }
  }

  // Heap root is the worst of the kept k; pop to get best-first order.
  const out = [];
  while (heap.length) {
    out.push(heap[0]);
    heap[0] = heap.pop();
    if (heap.length) siftDown(0);
  }
  return out.reverse();
}
```

The heap holds the k best words, with the root being the worst of them, so evicting when we exceed k drops the right one. Popping gives worst-to-best, so I reverse at the end. Time is O(n log k), space O(k) beyond the count maps.

[[GIST]] min-heap of size k, ties by first index

## Approach
Count each character's occurrences in one pass with a hash map, then walk the string a second time and return the index of the first character whose count is exactly one. If no such character exists, return -1.

## Code
```python
def first_unique_index(s):
    counts = {}
    for ch in s:
        counts[ch] = counts.get(ch, 0) + 1
    for i, ch in enumerate(s):
        if counts[ch] == 1:
            return i
    return -1
```

## Complexity
Time O(n), two passes over the string. Space O(k), where k is the number of distinct characters.
